<h2>The problem with reporting on traffic</h2>

<p>
  Early in my SEO work I spent a lot of time writing emails to clients and
  marketing managers explaining why a site was performing well even though the
  owner had Googled themselves and not seen their practice. Those conversations
  taught me something that shaped how I work: <strong>a report that cannot
  explain why a number moved is not a report, it's a chart</strong>.
</p>

<p>
  Ranking is a proxy. Sessions are a proxy. For a practice owner, the real
  question is whether the phone rang and whether the appointment got booked.
  If the measurement architecture can't connect organic work to those outcomes,
  every strategic conversation becomes an argument about proxies.
</p>

<h2>What I built</h2>

<h3>Standardized GTM architecture</h3>
<p>
  Tag containers across client and corporate domains had drifted into
  inconsistency &mdash; different naming, duplicate tags, conflicting triggers,
  and events that meant different things on different sites. I standardized the
  container architecture so that an event name means the same thing everywhere.
  This sounds like housekeeping. It is actually the precondition for any
  cross-portfolio analysis being trustworthy.
</p>

<h3>GA4 conversion modeling around real outcomes</h3>
<p>
  Conversions were defined around the actions tied to revenue: tracked phone
  calls, form submissions, and appointment bookings &mdash; not scroll depth or
  time on page. Where a practice used a third-party booking or call-tracking
  system, the integration had to be deliberate rather than assumed.
</p>

<h3>First-party attribution pipelines</h3>
<p>
  Third-party cookie decay and privacy changes have steadily degraded
  off-the-shelf attribution. I've built first-party pipelines so the connection
  between an organic session and a downstream outcome survives, rather than
  being quietly reassigned to direct traffic.
</p>

<h3>Looker Studio reporting with integrated sources</h3>
<p>
  For clients on dedicated SEO plans I build custom dashboards that pull
  multiple data sources into one view, so the conversation starts from a shared
  picture rather than three disagreeing exports.
</p>

<h2>The diagnostic habit</h2>

<p>
  When organic performance changes, I work through a consistent sequence before
  forming a hypothesis:
</p>

<ol>
  <li>Is this a measurement change or a real change? Tag breakage and
    reporting-side changes explain a surprising share of apparent cliffs.</li>
  <li>Is it broad or concentrated? A portfolio-wide shift and a single-page
    shift have entirely different causes.</li>
  <li>Did the SERP change, or did we? Feature changes, new competitors, and
    layout shifts move clicks without moving position.</li>
  <li>Did position change, or did click-through? GSC separates these, and
    conflating them sends you down the wrong remediation path.</li>
  <li>Is the conversion rate stable? Traffic down with conversions flat is a
    different problem than traffic flat with conversions down.</li>
  <li>What changed on the site, and when? Deploy and content timelines are
    usually the fastest route to the actual answer.</li>
</ol>

<p>
  The point of the sequence is to arrive at a recommendation that is backed by
  reasoning the client can follow. "Rankings dropped, we'll optimize" is not
  actionable. "Impressions held, CTR fell on these twelve pages after a SERP
  layout change, here's the title and schema response" is.
</p>
